		<div class="post" id="post-4202906">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,4202906)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4202906">
																		Keeps Me Coming Back
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kereea" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5267515); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5267515&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973755" alt="Kereea" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5267515)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kereea" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5267515); return false;" >Kereea</a></span> <span class="odate time_1554030755 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">31 Mar 2019 11:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4202906">
									

<p>This is one of the things that really drew me in and got me to make an account. Something about this script just feels OFF, and I mean that in a good was as an SCP supplement. It keeps you thinking. All the most important things&#8212;the ghost, getting the confession, etc, all happen offstage. Isabella's characterization is another, swinging wildly tho tbf she was drunk in her first scene. She confesses, she's sent away, she tries to kill her son for no obvious reason. It feels like something was cut out there.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This feels like someone tried to clean up an original play and perhaps wasn't as good a writer as they hoped. While without greater access to the actually non-existant text it's hard to tell, one really wonders why something as trite-seeming as this would draw a school drama club's attention even as a &quot;less violent Hamlet&quot; given Hamet's got a lot more going for it just in coherency of plot and character. And that's <em>with</em> the main character of Hamlet going mad/faking going mad as a major part of the play. Really this summary puts me more in mind of The Winter's Tale, a comedy, where fantastical things happen in order to resolve the problems started by the first act in a happier way, with Winter's Tale being saved by the strength of the full text which again, we do not have here and some of us have considered trying to tackle.</p>
<p>Then there's the title. This is a play called &quot;The Hanged King's Tragedy&quot; and yet it ends with the conventions of a comedy, with conflict resolved and a wedding to come. As the 701 article itself has us compare it to Hamlet, I cannot help but do that, and this being titled a tragedy with the ending it has really hooks you as the implication that something has gone very, very wrong, even as you just skim the plot with no intentions of performing it and risking the massacre. This also puts me in mind of The King in Yellow, eventually developed as a published play by a man with more guts than most, Thom Ryng. In addition to the comparisons between Caracosa (present domain of Hastur in the Chtulhu mythos) and Alagadda as noted by the expeditions listed in SCP-2264, the written play for the King in Yellow is an actual. darn. tragedy. And I cannot help but compare its plot to the stated plot here and wonder at where the latter may indeed help at reconstructing the former. <a href="https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Theatre/TheKingInYellow">Here's a link to the play's TvTropes article, for those interested in the basics</a>. The Stranger/Mask of Truth really puts me in mind as to the descriptions here of the Ambassador of Alagadda, which could have some interesting implications for both the &quot;true&quot; text of this play and the general Alagadda mythos as a whole.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<em>As a courtesy to our readers on mobile devices, please collapse long posts. ~Zyn</em>
<p>Again, glad to be here and commenting, this whole mess with Alagadda and the Hanged King's Tragedy are some of my fav SCPs and I keep coming back to them all the time.</p>

							</div>

								
